    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499820/assistant-professor-publicnonprofit-management": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Public/Nonprofit Management",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Public/Nonprofit Management Apply now Search #: 499820 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Hartford Categories: Faculty Tenure Track INTRODUCTION The School of Public Policy (SPP) at the University of Connecticut (UConn) seeks to fill a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor starting in August 2027. Candidates already appointed at the Assistant Professor level are encouraged to apply. We are seeking candidates with research and teaching interests in public and/or nonprofit management. The successful candidate is expected to demonstrate a strong research agenda in public and/or nonprofit management. The successful candidate’s primary teaching assignment will include core classes in the MPA and/or Nonprofit Management Certificate programs, such as courses in public management, project management, human resource management, and nonprofit management. The specific teaching assignment will be determined based on the interests of the candidate and the needs of the School and may include graduate and undergraduate classes. The successful candidate will demonstrate a deep commitment to student success and teaching excellence at the undergraduate and graduate levels, with a preference for candidates with professional experience in public or nonprofit management. The best candidates will also provide clear evidence of contributions to the diversity and excellence of the learning experience through teaching, research, and/or public engagement. ABOUT THE SCHOOL OF PUBLIC POLICY The School of Public Policy is home to a Master of Public Administration (MPA) program, an Executive Track MPA program (the Fellows program) offered in Hartford and in Stamford, and a Master of Public Policy (MPP) program. It also offers three graduate certificates: Nonprofit Management, Public Financial Management, and Leadership and Public Management. The School of Public Policy is located on the state-of-the-art campus in Hartford, Connecticut’s capital city. The School also offers classes in Stamford and Storrs. ABOUT THE UNIVERSITY OF CONNECTICUT Founded in 1881, UConn is the state’s flagship institution of higher education and includes a main campus in Storrs, CT, four regional campuses throughout the state, and 13 Schools and Colleges, including a Law School in Hartford, and Medical and Dental Schools at the UConn Health campus in Farmington. The University has approximately 10,000 faculty and staff and 32,000 students, including nearly 24,000 undergraduates and over 8,000 graduate and professional students. UConn is a Carnegie Foundation R1 (highest research activity) institution, among the top public universities in the nation. Through research, teaching, service, and outreach, UConn embraces diversity and cultivates leadership, integrity, and engaged citizenship in its students, faculty, staff, and alumni. UConn promotes the health and well-being of citizens by enhancing the social, economic, cultural, and natural environments of the state and beyond. The University serves as a beacon of academic and research excellence as well as a center for innovation and social service to communities. UConn is a leader in many scholarly, research, and innovation areas. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Earned Ph.D. in public affairs, public administration, management (with a nonprofit management focus), organizational behavior (with a nonprofit management focus), sociology (with an organizational theory and management focus), or a related field. ABD candidates will be considered with the expectation that the Ph.D. is completed before the start date. Candidates already appointed at the Assistant Professor level are also encouraged to apply. A strong record of or strong potential for research excellence in the areas of public management and/or nonprofit management. Demonstrated written and verbal communication skills. Ability to teach core public management and/or nonprofit management courses in the MPA and/or Nonprofit Management Certificate programs. Demonstrated experience contributing through scholarship, teaching, and/or public engagement to the diversity and excellence of the learning experience. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Public or nonprofit management professional experience, and/or ability to teach Executive Track courses. A record of, or potential for, obtaining external research funding. Evidence of a commitment to student success and teaching excellence, such as positive teaching evaluations, innovative courses and instruction methods, and/or mentoring students in research and/or professional development. Interest in engaging with local, state, and national public service and/or nonprofit organizations in related areas of expertise. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $105,000 to $115,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves and Engagement & Learning headers at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the American Association of University Professors (AAUP) bargaining unit and look for information specifically identified as AAUP, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track position with an anticipated start date of August 23, 2027, with a possibility of starting in January 2027. The successful candidate’s academic appointment will be in the School of Public Policy located at the Hartford Campus. The successful candidate’s ordinary teaching workload could include teaching at the Hartford campus, at the Stamford campus, at UConn’s main campus in Storrs, and/or online. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499820 , and submit the following application materials: A cover letter of no more than two pages specifically addressing your credentials relative to the minimum and preferred qualifications listed above, Current curriculum vitae , One-page teaching statement reflective of your teaching philosophy and interests, Recent teaching evaluations (if available), Contact information for three references (email, phone number, and mailing address) who can comment on research and teaching capabilities and accomplishments, and One-page commitment to diversity statement (as related to broadening participation, integrating multicultural experiences in instruction and research, and pedagogical techniques to meet the needs of diverse learning styles, etc.). Review of applicants will begin on October 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For more information, please visit the School of Public Policy website . Any questions about the position may be sent to Deneen Hatmaker , Professor, School of Public Policy. At the University of Connecticut, our commitment to excellence is complemented by our commitment to building a culturally diverse community. This position will be filled subject the budgetary approval. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, gender, gender identity or expression, sexual orientation, national origin, genetics, disability, age, or veteran status. The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO Employer. Advertised: Sep 01 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499844/student-engagement-research-instruction-librarian-1-or-2": {
      "title": "Student Engagement Research & Instruction Librarian 1 or 2",
      "description": "Student Engagement Research & Instruction Librarian 1 or 2 Apply now Search #: 499844 Work type: Full-time Location: School of Law Categories: Libraries JOB SUMMARY Reporting to the Associate Director of Research, Instruction, and Scholarship, or her/his designee, the Student Engagement Research & Instruction Librarian is responsible for implementing library services directly aimed at supporting student success and providing general and in-depth research, reference, and instructional services in all areas of the law to the students, faculty and staff of the University of Connecticut School of Law and the University of Connecticut. The ideal candidate will demonstrate creativity, strategic thinking, and adaptability, embracing change and helping to shape the future of library services through innovative approaches and continuous improvement. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES INCLUDE: Develop and deliver programs, services, and events that promote and support student success, including but not limited to individual or group research consultations, training, outreach events, and library orientation. Coordinate strategic marketing of library services, resources, and spaces including managing the Law Library's blog, creating web content, displays, digital signage, and other promotional materials. Hire, train, and supervise student employees who staff the library Information Desk. Serve as a liaison to student groups and student-edited publications. Assess student research needs and satisfaction with library services and solicit and evaluate recommendations for improvements through surveys, focus groups, and other assessment methods. Provide research and instructional support for day and evening division students, including course-integrated presentations, formal and informal legal information instruction, and similar activities. Contribute to faculty research service by conducting in-depth legal and interdisciplinary research, and preparing and creating bibliographies, literature reviews, memoranda, and other research deliverables. Provide reference assistance in person and virtually, including regular participation in evening and weekend rotations. Develop instructional materials, including research guides and tutorials, using a range of formats and delivery methods. Select material for inclusion in the library collection and actively participate in collection development, assessment, retention, and deselection. Participate in regular meetings, committees, and campus events, as appropriate. Participate in professional development opportunities. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A graduate degree in Library and Information Science from an ALA accredited program. Demonstrated analytical and problem-solving skills. Demonstrated strong interpersonal, verbal, and written communication skills. Ability to make effective presentations to individuals and/or groups. Ability to work collegially with diverse groups. Evidence of participation in professional development. Ability to work evenings and/or weekend hours. Additional Minimum Qualifications for Librarian 2 appointment: Minimum of three years of related professional experience in a library. Demonstrated ability to set priorities and juggle multiple tasks. Demonstrated ability to introduce and establish change. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS JD from an ABA accredited program. One (1) to three (3) years or more of related experience. Experience providing instruction in formal and informal settings. Previous experience in a law library. Reference desk experience in an academic library. Experience with legal research platforms, such as Westlaw, Lexis, and Bloomberg Law. Experience with library and instructional technology. Demonstrated ability to work successfully in a team-based environment. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $67,666 to $87,966. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA) and look for information specifically identified for UCPEA, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time position located at the Law School. The job classification as a Librarian 1 (P4) or Librarian 2 (P5) and salary will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s training and work experience. Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. The successful candidate must be willing and able to work evenings and/or weekend hours. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499844 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting is scheduled to be removed at 11:55 p.m. Eastern time on October 30, 2026. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Sep 28 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Oct 30 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499734/open-rank-clinical-professor-legal-practice-program": {
      "title": "Open Rank Clinical Professor - Legal Practice Program",
      "description": "Open Rank Clinical Professor - Legal Practice Program Apply now Search #: 499734 Work type: Full-time Location: School of Law Categories: Faculty Non Tenure Track INTRODUCTION The University of Connecticut (UConn) School of Law seeks candidates for an open-rank clinical faculty position in its Legal Practice Program (“LP Program”), beginning in the fall of 2027. Appointment may be made at the rank of Assistant, Associate, or Full Clinical Professor of Law, commensurate with the successful candidate’s qualifications and experience. The LP Program at UConn School of Law is an integrated seven-credit first-year program that provides experiential training in legal writing, legal research, oral advocacy, interviewing, counseling, negotiation, and professional identity formation. This position affords the opportunity to teach Legal Practice during the fall and spring semesters; design and refine curriculum, simulations, assignments, and assessments that advance students’ lawyering skills; and mentor students as they develop as legal professionals. Faculty teach through a combination of classroom instruction and simulated client representations, culminating in an innovative spring-semester skills capstone. The program encourages thoughtful integration of emerging technologies—including legal research platforms and generative artificial intelligence—to enhance student learning while cultivating ethical judgment and professional responsibility needed to use these tools effectively in legal practice. Although LP faculty collaborate closely on curriculum design and program development, each faculty member exercises substantial autonomy in organizing and teaching their course, selecting instructional approaches, and developing classroom materials consistent with program learning outcomes. UConn School of Law is a top-ranked public law school in the Northeast, offering a professional education and scholarly environment of the highest quality. The School is committed to building and supporting a vibrant, multicultural, and diverse community of students, faculty, and staff. Its beautiful Gothic Revival campus is located in the West End of Hartford, a few miles from the state capitol and courts, as well as the headquarters of leading insurance companies and other major corporations. The School has both day and evening divisions and offers the JD (Juris Doctor) degree, LLM (Master of Laws) degrees, and the SJD (Doctor of Laws) degree, as well as several dual degree programs. The UConn Law faculty includes leading scholars, experienced practitioners, and internationally known experts in a wide range of fields. The Law School hosts three student journals, over forty student organizations, extensive clinical and public service for the surrounding communities, and one of the largest law libraries in the world. Founded in 1881, UConn is a Land Grant and Sea Grant institution and member of the Space Grant Consortium. It is the state’s flagship institution of higher education and includes a main campus in Storrs, CT, four regional campuses throughout the state, and 13 Schools and Colleges, including a Law School in Hartford, and Medical and Dental Schools at the UConn Health campus in Farmington. The University has approximately 10,000 faculty and staff and 32,000 students, including nearly 24,000 undergraduates and over 8,000 graduate and professional students. UConn is a Carnegie Foundation R1 (highest research activity) institution, among the top 25 public universities in the nation. Through research, teaching, service, and outreach, UConn embraces diversity and cultivates leadership, integrity, and engaged citizenship in its students, faculty, staff, and alumni. UConn promotes the health and well-being of citizens by enhancing the social, economic, cultural, and natural environments of the state and beyond. The University serves as a beacon of academic and research excellence as well as a center for innovation and social service to communities. UConn is a leader in many scholarly, research, and innovation areas. Today, the path forward includes exciting opportunities. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS J.D. from an ABA-accredited law school. Excellent legal research, writing, and analytical skills. Demonstrated excellence in written and oral communication. At least three years of legal practice experience. A demonstrated commitment to advancing diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging in the workplace, legal academy, and/or profession. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Record of excellence in teaching legal writing, legal skills, or experiential courses. Significant experience in legal writing, client interviewing, counseling, negotiation, and oral advocacy. Demonstrated commitment to evidence-based teaching and learning, including a willingness to develop and refine pedagogy. Demonstrated commitment to continuous curricular improvement and innovation in legal education. Experience using or teaching with legal technology, including electronic research platforms, practice technologies, and/or generative AI tools, and a commitment to helping students develop the skills and ethical judgment necessary to use these technologies effectively, ethically, and professionally. National or regional engagement with organizations related to legal writing, experiential education, legal pedagogy, or the legal profession. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $100,000 to $160,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves and Engagement & Learning headers at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, non-tenure-track position that is eligible for five-year, presumptively renewable long-term contracts. The initial appointment will be for one year (for those on track toward a long-term contract) or five years (for those appointed with a long-term contract) depending on experience. Renewals will be based on performance. Candidates should expect to work at the Law School located in Hartford, Connecticut. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499734 and submit the following application materials: A letter of interest Curriculum vitae Writing sample Teaching statement Names and contact information for three (3) references Any questions about application materials may be directed to the appointments committee chair, Goldburn P. Maynard Jr., at goldburn.maynard@uconn.edu Review of applications will begin immediately, with those submitted by September 30, 2026 receiving priority consideration. The position will remain open until filled and is subject to budgetary approval. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Jul 22 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499737/open-rank-clinical-professor-of-lawdirector-of-academic-success-and-bar-prep": {
      "title": "Open Rank Clinical Professor of Law/Director of Academic Success and Bar Prep",
      "description": "Open Rank Clinical Professor of Law/Director of Academic Success and Bar Prep Apply now Search #: 499737 Work type: Full-time Location: School of Law Categories: Faculty Non Tenure Track INTRODUCTION The University of Connecticut (UConn) School of Law seeks candidates for an open-rank clinical faculty position as Director of Academic Success and Bar Preparation beginning in the fall of 2027. This is a long-term contract faculty position within the Law School’s Professional Skills Faculty. Appointment may be made at the Assistant, Associate, or Full rank level, depending on qualifications and experience. In collaboration with the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs, the Director will provide strategic leadership for the Law School’s established academic success and bar readiness program, assessing, enhancing, and reimagining program components as appropriate to support students and graduates from matriculation through bar admission. The Director will work with faculty, administrators, staff, alumni, and external partners to identify and support students at risk of academic or bar exam underperformance; to design and deliver workshops, programs, and resources to promote student achievement, professional development, bar readiness, and licensure success; and to align the program with evolving accreditation standards and licensure requirements, including the NextGen bar. The Director will teach one academic success course each fall and spring semester, provide individualized advising and coaching, and develop and teach bar preparation and related courses, with substantial opportunity to shape the structure and delivery of the Law School’s bar readiness program. UConn School of Law is a top-ranked public law school in the Northeast, offering a professional education and scholarly environment of the highest quality. The School is committed to building and supporting a vibrant, multicultural, and diverse community of students, faculty, and staff. Its beautiful Gothic Revival campus is located in the West End of Hartford, a few miles from the state capitol and courts, as well as the headquarters of leading insurance companies and other major corporations. The School has both day and evening divisions and offers the JD (Juris Doctor) degree, LLM (Master of Laws) degrees, and the SJD (Doctor of Laws) degree, as well as several dual degree programs. The UConn Law faculty includes leading scholars, experienced practitioners, and internationally known experts in a wide range of fields. The Law School hosts three student journals, over forty student organizations, extensive clinical and public service for the surrounding communities, and one of the largest law libraries in the world. Founded in 1881, UConn is a Land Grant and Sea Grant institution and member of the Space Grant Consortium. It is the state’s flagship institution of higher education and includes a main campus in Storrs, CT, four regional campuses throughout the state, and 13 Schools and Colleges, including a Law School in Hartford, and Medical and Dental Schools at the UConn Health campus in Farmington. The University has approximately 10,000 faculty and staff and 32,000 students, including nearly 24,000 undergraduates and over 8,000 graduate and professional students. UConn is a Carnegie Foundation R1 (highest research activity) institution, among the top 25 public universities in the nation. Through research, teaching, service, and outreach, UConn embraces diversity and cultivates leadership, integrity, and engaged citizenship in its students, faculty, staff, and alumni. UConn promotes the health and well-being of citizens by enhancing the social, economic, cultural, and natural environments of the state and beyond. The University serves as a beacon of academic and research excellence as well as a center for innovation and social service to communities. UConn is a leader in many scholarly, research, and innovation areas. Today, the path forward includes exciting opportunities. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A J.D. degree from an ABA-accredited law school. Excellent written, oral, interpersonal, and organizational skills. A demonstrated commitment to advancing diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging in the legal academy and/or profession. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Minimum of two years of relevant experience, preferably in academic success, teaching, academic support/skills programming, bar preparation, student affairs, advising, or related areas at the law school level. Demonstrated understanding of the evolving attorney licensure landscape, including the NextGen Bar Exam. Demonstrated ability to foster an inclusive, supportive, and student-centered learning environment that responds effectively to the needs of students from diverse backgrounds and supports the academic, professional, and personal development of all students. Demonstrated administrative and program-assessment abilities. Experience teaching legal writing. Previous legal practice experience. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $100,000 to $160,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves and Engagement & Learning headers at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is classified as Law School Faculty. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, non-tenure-track position that is eligible for five-year, presumptively renewable long-term contracts. The initial appointment will be for one year (for those on track toward a long-term contract) or five years (for those appointed with a long-term contract) depending on experience. Renewals will be based on performance. Candidates should expect to work at the Law School located in Hartford, Connecticut. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499737 and submit the following application materials: A letter of interest Curriculum vitae Names and contact information for three (3) references Writing sample Any questions about application materials may be directed to the appointments committee chair, Goldburn P. Maynard Jr., at goldburn.maynard@uconn.edu Review of applications will begin immediately, with those applications submitted by September 30, 2026, receiving priority consideration. The position will remain open until filled and is subject to budgetary approval. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Jul 22 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
