    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499733/thomas-f-gallivan-chair-of-property-law": {
      "title": "Thomas F Gallivan Chair of Property Law",
      "description": "Thomas F Gallivan Chair of Property Law Apply now Search #: 499733 Work type: Full-time Location: School of Law Categories: Faculty Tenure Track, Legal THOMAS F. GALLIVAN, JR. CHAIR OF REAL PROPERTY LAW The University of Connecticut (UConn) School of Law seeks applications from lateral candidates for the Thomas F. Gallivan, Jr. Chair of Real Property Law. This is a tenured faculty position reserved for an outstanding scholar with an international or national reputation for excellence in property law scholarship, teaching, and service. Candidates for the Gallivan Chair should have an ambitious research agenda that seeks to shape contemporary conversations in property, land use and/or real estate law. We welcome applications from candidates pursuing innovative work within these areas that involve issues such as housing, local government, environmental law, and resiliency. The Gallivan Chair will be expected to provide vision and leadership through scholarly research and engagement at major national and international conferences and symposia, along with developing innovative Law School program and academic offerings. The Chair will also be expected to engage with members of the Connecticut real property bar. The position provides discretionary endowment funds to support research, to build relationships with property scholars, practitioners, and policymakers, and to engage with the broader legal community. The Gallivan Chair presents a unique opportunity to collaborate with the Center for Energy & Environmental Law, the Connecticut Institute for Resilience and Climate Adaptation (CIRCA), and other partners throughout the University. The UConn School of Law is especially interested in candidates who will add to the diversity of our faculty and community. We welcome applications from members of underrepresented groups and other candidates with experiences, backgrounds, and viewpoints that will enrich the diversity of our institution. UConn School of Law is a top-ranked public law school in the Northeast, offering a professional education and scholarly environment of the highest quality. The School is committed to building and supporting a vibrant, multicultural, and diverse community of students, faculty, and staff. Its beautiful Gothic Revival campus is located in the West End of Hartford, a few miles from the state capital and courts, as well as the headquarters of leading insurance companies and other major corporations. The School has both day and evening divisions and offers the JD (Juris Doctor) degree, LLM (Master of Laws) degrees, and the SJD (Doctor of Laws) degree, as well as several dual degree programs. The UConn Law faculty includes leading scholars, experienced practitioners, and internationally known experts in a wide range of fields. The Law School hosts three student journals, over forty student organizations, extensive clinical and public service for the surrounding communities, and one of the largest law libraries in the world. Founded in 1881, UConn is a Land Grant and Sea Grant institution and member of the Space Grant Consortium. It is the state’s flagship institution of higher education and includes a main campus in Storrs, CT, four regional campuses throughout the state, and 13 Schools and Colleges, including a Law School in Hartford, and Medical and Dental Schools at the UConn Health campus in Farmington. The University has approximately 10,000 faculty and staff and 32,000 students, including nearly 24,000 undergraduates and over 8,000 graduate and professional students. UConn is a Carnegie Foundation R1 (highest research activity) institution, among the top 25 public universities in the nation. Through research, teaching, service, and outreach, UConn embraces diversity and cultivates leadership, integrity, and engaged citizenship in its students, faculty, staff, and alumni. UConn promotes the health and well-being of citizens by enhancing the social, economic, cultural, and natural environments of the state and beyond. The University serves as a beacon of academic and research excellence as well as a center for innovation and social service to communities. UConn is a leader in many scholarly, research, and innovation areas. Today, the path forward includes exciting opportunities. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS A JD degree or equivalent terminal degree in a related field. Qualifications for appointment at the rank of Full Professor with tenure. A distinguished record of high-impact scholarship in property law, land use, real estate law, or closely related areas. Demonstrated excellence in classroom teaching and a commitment to student mentorship. Willingness to engage with members of the Connecticut bar. A demonstrated commitment to advancing diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging in the workplace, legal academy, and/or profession. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience leading academic centers, programs, or policy initiatives. Proven track record of bridging academic theory with legal practice or public policy. Experience in securing and managing external research funding. Interest in developing new academic offerings appropriate for legal and other professional communities, such as planners, municipal officials, and others. Interest in elevating the scholarly profile and professional engagement of colleagues across the property law field. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $250,000 to $300,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves and Engagement & Learning headers at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, 9-month appointment for a tenured-at-hire position. Lateral applicants must meet Law School and University requirements for appointment at the rank of Full Professor with tenure. Salary and rank will be highly competitive and commensurate with background, qualifications, and experience. Candidates should expect to work at the Law School located in Hartford, Connecticut. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499733 and submit the following application materials: A letter of interest Curriculum vitae Scholarship statement Teaching statement Sample journal articles or books Names and contact information for three references Any questions about application materials may be directed to the appointments committee chair, Goldburn P. Maynard Jr., at goldburn.maynard@uconn.edu Review of applications will begin immediately, with those applications submitted by September 30, 2026, receiving priority consideration. The position will remain open until filled and is subject to budgetary approval. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status Advertised: Jul 20 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499822/assistant-associate-or-full-professor-addictions-and-behavioral-health-focus": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor - Addictions and Behavioral Health Focus",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor - Addictions and Behavioral Health Focus Apply now Search #: 499822 Work type: Full-time Location: School of Social Work Categories: Faculty Tenure Track JOB SUMMARY The University of Connecticut School of Social Work is pleased to invite applications for a full-time (9-month appointment), open-rank, tenure-line position. We seek a highly motivated, productive, and collaborative scholar committed to advancing research and education in addictions and behavioral health. We welcome applicants with expertise in intervention research, particularly those developing, evaluating, and implementing technology-based interventions. The UConn School of Social Work is a national leader in graduate social work education with a tradition of educating and serving diverse communities. We are ranked in the top 11% of schools of social work nationally and number one in Connecticut. The school’s research doctorate is the only public social work Ph.D. program in the region. The School's research productivity is on an upward trajectory. Our externally funded research portfolio exceeds $70 million, and the National Science Foundation's (NSF) Higher Education Research and Development Survey ranked UConn Social Work No. 7 among public schools of social work and No. 11 nationally in 2024 research expenditures. Areas of distinction include child welfare and child well-being; behavioral health, trauma, and violence prevention and reduction; aging; international social work and human rights; immigrant and refugee studies; social and health disparities; diversity and cultural competence; and policy analysis, advocacy, and community organizing. The school is also home to the Innovations Institute , a nationally recognized interdisciplinary implementation and translational research center. We are committed to fostering an inclusive, collegial, and supportive faculty environment. The UConn School of Social Work is located on the vibrant downtown Hartford regional campus. This neighborhood campus includes other UConn graduate and undergraduate academic programs and research centers, cultural institutions, and state and city government offices. This situates us ideally for carrying out our central academic mission and provides enhanced opportunities for building upon our existing community collaborations and expanding to new ones within a multicultural capital city. UConn is a Land Grant and Sea Grant institution and a member of the Space Grant Consortium, founded in 1881. It is the state’s flagship institution of higher education and includes a main campus in Storrs, CT, four regional campuses throughout the state, and 13 Schools and Colleges, including a Law School in Hartford, and Medical and Dental Schools at the UConn Health campus in Farmington. The University has approximately 10,000 faculty and staff and 32,000 students, including nearly 24,000 undergraduates and over 8,000 graduate and professional students. UConn is a Carnegie Foundation R1 (highest research activity) institution, among the top 25 public universities in the nation. Through research, teaching, service, and outreach, UConn embraces diversity and cultivates leadership, integrity, and engaged citizenship in its students, faculty, staff, and alumni. UConn promotes the health and well-being of citizens by enhancing the social, economic, cultural, and natural environments of the state and beyond. UConn serves as a beacon of academic and research excellence as well as a center for innovation and social service to communities. UConn is a leader in many scholarly, research, and innovation areas. Today, the path forward includes exciting opportunities and notable challenges. Record numbers of undergraduate applications and support for student success have enabled the University to become highly selective. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Maintain an active research and publication agenda. Seek and acquire external funding. Teach in BSW, MSW, and/or Ph.D. programs, as appropriate, and demonstrate effective instruction. Advise and mentor students. Serve on doctoral student committees, as appropriate, and participate in University, School, professional, local, and national level service. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Ph.D. in social work or a related discipline. Demonstrated excellence in scholarship, external funding, teaching, and mentoring. Demonstrated experience with and commitment to team science and cross-disciplinary inquiry. Demonstrated experience with and commitment to social work values. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS M.S.W. degree. Experience in social work practice. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $90,000 to $135,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves and Engagement & Learning headers at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the American Association of University Professors (AAUP) bargaining unit and look for information specifically identified as AAUP, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS This is a full-time, 9-month, tenure-track position with an anticipated start date of August 23, 2027. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499822 and submit the following application materials: Current curriculum vitae . Cover letter that comments on the relevant minimum and preferred qualifications. Research statement that describes the candidate's research and extramural funding trajectory and explains how the candidate’s research agenda aligns with the priorities of this position. Representative samples of up to three (3) publications . Names and contact information of three (3) references . Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. Applicants can address questions by email to: Jennifer Manuel, PhD Gio Ianconno, PhD Search Committee Co-Chairs University of Connecticut School of Social Work 38 Prospect Street Hartford, CT 06103 sswsearch@uconn.edu References will not be contacted without the prior permission of the candidate. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position is filled. At the University of Connecticut, our commitment to excellence is complemented by our commitment to building a culturally diverse community. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Sep 02 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
    },
    "https://jobs.hr.uconn.edu/en-us/job/499876/uconn-hartford-student-activities-coordinator-student-services-program-coordinator": {
      "title": "UConn Hartford Student Activities Coordinator (Student Services Program Coordinator)",
      "description": "UConn Hartford Student Activities Coordinator (Student Services Program Coordinator) Apply now Search #: 499876 Work type: Full-time Location: UConn Hartford Categories: Student Programs and Services JOB SUMMARY The University of Connecticut Hartford Campus is seeking a full-time Student Activities Coordinator. Under the direction of the Director of Event Services, this position will support student activities and leadership development for UConn Hartford undergraduate students. This individual will advise and provide leadership to the UConn Hartford Undergraduate Student Government; support undergraduate student organizations at the Hartford campus; and provide students with leadership development and experiential learning opportunities both on and off campus in collaboration with cultural, political, social, and economic institutions in the surrounding metropolitan area. The successful candidate for this position will have the ability to work as part of a team to efficiently manage a detailed portfolio; a strong ethical sense; good judgment; and a deep commitment to antiracist practice, equity and inclusion. The Student Activities Coordinator will work closely with other departments and staff on campus. DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Advises the Undergraduate Student Government (USG), including the executive board and committees, in planning and managing their policies, programs and general operations, in accordance with University policies pertaining to University Tier III organizations. Serves as the campus financial oversight of the USG budget, including training, purchasing, transaction protocol procedures and inventory of goods. Monitors student organization registration process; reviews new registered student organization (RSO) applications; develops, implements, and tracks completion of training requirements. Serves as a liaison between RSOs and the Department of Student Activities at Storrs. Plans, develops, and implements methods and techniques of informing/ educating/ training/ recognizing student leaders; develops and disseminates informational topics to students, faculty, and staff. Advises and assists registered student organizations in planning and managing their policies, programs, and general operations; connects student organizations with community resources, service learning, leadership development, and career development opportunities while respecting the autonomy legally accorded such organizations. Co-manage s Orientation operations, including staffing , ensuring seamless execution, precise schedule adherence, and an engaging experience f or incoming students and families. Develops and coordinates inclusive programs that build community, cultivate a sense of belonging, and enhance the student experience by creating opportunities for self-reflection, meaningful action, and collaborative engagement across diverse identities on campus and with the local community. Plans and assists with campus - wide events, such as Involvement Fairs, Volunteer Fairs, Convocation and End of Semester Celebrations . Teaches at least one section of the First-Year Experience class every academic year . Serves as a liaison with the other regional campus student activities staff . Performs related duties as assigned . MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS Bachelor’s degree in an appropriate field and three to four years of professional experience in a responsible position related to student activities, or similar work which demonstrates knowledge of leadership development programs and student organization advising. Demonstrated effective organizational, operational, and programming skills. Demonstrated knowledge of planning and implementing Orientation, first-year/transfer/family/transitional programs, and student leadership trainings. Demonstrated interpersonal, advising and communication skills. Demonstrated knowledge of budgeting and fiscal management. Demonstrated experience developing inclusive student programming, leading outreach, or facilitating dialogue that engages diverse student populations and fosters community partnerships. Stated or demonstrated interest in and commitment to working at a large, diverse, and urban campus. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Master’s degree in Higher Education Administration, Student Personnel or related field. Experience developing inclusive and high-impact educational, leadership, and student-engaged programming. Demonstrated knowledge of practices of antiracism, equity, and inclusion. Demonstrated ability to effectively manage multiple projects and programs. COMPENSATION AND BENEFITS The hiring range for this position is $63,000 to $68,000. An offer of employment, if made, will be commensurate with the successful candidate’s skill and experience, as determined by the University. For more information about benefits and other offerings available to University employees, we encourage you to review the Human Resources website and explore the sections under the Benefits & Leaves header at the top of the page. Under Benefits & Leaves, select Benefits to access an overview as well as additional information on topics such as paid time off, benefits, retirement, and other resources. As you explore these pages, please note that this position is covered by the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA) and look for information specifically identified for UCPEA, as provisions may vary by employee group. APPOINTMENT TERMS Other rights, terms, and conditions of employment are contained in the collective bargaining agreement between the University of Connecticut and the University of Connecticut Professional Employees Association (UCPEA). The selected candidate must have a willingness to work irregular and flexible hours; evening and weekend hours are required. TERMS AND CONDITIONS OF EMPLOYMENT Employment of the successful candidate is contingent upon the successful completion of a pre-employment criminal background check. TO APPLY Please apply online at Faculty and Staff Positions , Search #499876 to upload a resume , cover letter , and contact information for three (3) professional references . Applicants must clearly demonstrate how they meet the stated minimum qualifications, and any preferred qualifications they may possess, in their application materials. This job posting will remain open until filled. All employees are subject to adherence to the State Code of Ethics . All members of the University of Connecticut are expected to exhibit appreciation of, and contribute to, an inclusive, respectful, and diverse environment for the University community. The University of Connecticut aspires to create a community built on collaboration and belonging and has actively sought to create an inclusive culture within the workforce. The success of the University is dependent on the willingness of our diverse employee and student populations to share their rich perspectives and backgrounds in a respectful manner. This makes it essential for each member of our community to feel secure and welcomed and to thoroughly understand and believe that their ideas are respected by all. We strongly respect each individual employee’s unique experiences and perspectives and encourage all members of the community to do the same. All applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, ethnicity, religion, age, sex, marital status, national origin, ancestry, sexual orientation, genetic information, physical or mental disabilities, veteran’s status, status as a victim of domestic violence and/or sexual assault and/or trafficking in persons as defined by Connecticut law, prior conviction of a crime, workplace hazards to the reproductive systems, or gender identity or expression . The University of Connecticut is an AA/EEO employer including for Disability and Veteran status. Advertised: Oct 14 2026 Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: Back to search results Apply now Refer a friend Whatsapp Facebook LinkedIn Email App"
